Alloy: winegstreamer: decode to system memory when the device has no NV12 textures, and offer NV12 last.

Unreal Engine 5's Electra player decodes H.264 with this decoder the Windows way: it hands the decoder a DXGI device
manager and asks for NV12. DXMT 0.80 cannot create NV12 textures, so the sample allocator failed on the first
frame and the decoder returned E_INVALIDARG from every ProcessOutput: 0 of 48 frames of a test clip decoded.
SET_D3D_MANAGER now checks the device first and, when it cannot create NV12 textures, declines with E_NOTIMPL, as a
decoder without Direct3D support does; the caller then decodes to system memory (48 of 48 frames). Wine's own Source
Reader ignores the result and its topology loader only goes on with Direct3D when it succeeds.

CrossOver hides NV12 from the output types on macOS (CW HACK 26265), and in its place listed YV12 twice. A caller
that looks for NV12 in the list found nothing. NV12 is now listed last: callers that take the first type still get
YV12, as with CrossOver's change, and callers that look for NV12 find it.

